.TH CBMAP_INIT 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_init \- create an ordered map with defaults into an already declared handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_init(hm_name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates an ordered map with the default allocator and the default order
for the declared key type, and assigns it to hm_name. The caller must
declare the handle first, with cbmap_declare(3) or
cbmap_declare_scoped(3). The key type and the value type are not arguments
here; the macro reads them from the companion type variables that the
declare macro created.

It is the narrowest of the init macros. cbmap_init_mp(3) adds an
allocator, cbmap_init_cc(3) adds a comparison procedure, and
cbmap_init_full(3) takes both. The one-step equivalents are
cbmap_construct(3) and cbmap_construct_scoped(3).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  The map handle that the caller declared earlier, which receives
         the new map
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro expands to a statement, not to an expression.
.fi
.SH "ORDERING"
.nf
The container is an AVL tree, so it keeps its elements in key order and a
walk visits them in ascending key order, as cbmap_begin_iter(3)
describes. The declared key type picks the order: a signed integer
comparison for the signed integer types, an unsigned comparison for the
unsigned types and pointers, a numeric comparison for the floating-point
types and a lexicographic byte comparison for a string key.
cbmap_create_full(3) spells all of these out, including where a NaN key
sorts.

A key type that the map doesn't recognize, such as a struct, is compared
by the raw representation of the key, which reads the padding bytes and
orders a pointer member by its address. Use cbmap_init_cc(3) for such a
key type.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through _ccol_fatal_err() if the creation
fails, and it uses the default allocator.

Only the key type reaches the container; the declared value type is read
by the type-inferred macros alone.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cbmap_declare(sorted, int, char *);
cbmap_init(sorted);
int k = 42;
cbmap_insert(sorted, k, "hello");
k = 10;
cbmap_insert(sorted, k, "world");    /* The walk visits 10 before 42 */
cbmap_destroy(sorted);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_init_mp (3),
.BR cbmap_init_cc (3),
.BR cbmap_init_full (3),
.BR cbmap_declare (3),
.BR cbmap_declare_scoped (3),
.BR cbmap_construct (3),
.BR cbmap_begin_iter (3),
.BR cbmap_create_full (3),
.BR cbmap_destroy (3)
